
General notes about installing plugins on Debian
------------------------------------------------

  After installing new plugins, it is necessary to run the Rake command
to update Redmine database schemas on each instance of Redmine where
you use the plugin.

  Debian's Redmine packaging supports multiple instances of Redmine on the
same host.  These are the exact steps:

1) Install the plugin with apt

2) Create a symlink for each ${INSTANCE} from

      /var/lib/redmine/${INSTANCE}/plugins/

          pointing to /usr/share/redmine/plugins/${THIS_PLUGIN}

   Example command:

      ln -s /usr/share/redmine/plugins/${THIS_PLUGIN} \
         /var/lib/redmine/${INSTANCE}/plugins/

3) Ensure all per-instance directories have been created correctly
   under /var/lib/redmine/${INSTANCE}/

   Using the Debian script to create instances should do everything
   correctly.  For example:

      /usr/share/redmine/bin/redmine-instances create test01

4) Ensure all per-instance directories have the correct owner and
   permissions.  Some directories, such as the cache directory for
   each instance, must be writeable by the user running the Redmine
   process.
   
   On Apache2 / Passenger-based installations, the www-data
   user must be able to write to the cache directory

   There appears to be some confusion about which directory should be used.
   The exact directory names have varied in different versions of the
   Debian Redmine multi-tenancy packaging scheme.

   Older versions of Redmine used an Alias or similar directive
   in the web server VirtualHost configuration to map /assets/ or
   /plugin_assets/ into the web site.  Please check carefully whether
   these Alias directives are correct or whether they should be
   removed from the web server configuration completely depending
   on the Redmine version.

   An example command to ensure permissions are correct:

     chown -R www-data:www-data \
          /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/assets                \
          /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/assets/plugin_assets  \
          /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/plugin_assets

5) Run the Rake command with the name of the Instance, e.g.

      sudo -u www-data \
          RAILS_ENV=production \
          REDMINE_INSTANCE=${INSTANCE} \
          SCHEMA=/dev/null \
              rake redmine:plugins

6) Run the Rake command for plugin_assets:

      sudo -u www-data \
          RAILS_ENV=production \
          REDMINE_INSTANCE=${INSTANCE} \
          SCHEMA=/dev/null \
              rake assets:precompile

7) Re-run the Debian package maintainer script:

      sudo dpkg-reconfigure redmine

8) After doing the above steps for every Redmine instance where you
   symlinked the plugin, restart the process where Redmine is hosted.
   For example, restart Apache with

       systemctl restart apache2

9) Look at the Administration / Plugins list to verify the plugin
   was detected by Redmine.

10) Check /var/log/redmine/${INSTANCE}/production.log, the web server logs,
   SQL database logs and system logs / journalctl for any errors
   when you first start using the plugin.

Reporting errors
----------------

Please report any errors in both the Redmine Forum and the
Debian Bug Tracking System (BTS).  For example, to report
a bug to Debian:

         To: submit@bugs.debian.org
         Subject: Redmine missing task_done.png

         Message body:

             Package: redmine
             Version: 6.0.5+ds-1

             I was looking at Redmine production.log and I saw errors
             about a missing file task_done.png 


Troubleshooting plugins on Debian
---------------------------------

  Make sure you have the latest version of the package from either
debian.org or debify.org

  Look at the JavaScript console in the web browser.

  Look in the developer console of the web browser, network tab,
to see if it is failing to retrieve any files.

  Look at the /var/log/redmine/${INSTANCE}/production.log

  Look for error responses (e.g. 404) in the web server access.log

  Look for any errors in the web server error.log file

  Look in the cache directories for each Redmine instance and verify the 
JavaScript and CSS stylesheets were correctly copied from the plugin.
There appears to be some confusion about which directory should be used.
The exact directory names have varied in different versions of the
Debian Redmine multi-tenancy packaging scheme.
Look in each of these locations to verify the directory ownership,
write permissions and presence of the files for the installed plugins.

      ls /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/plugin_assets
      ls /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/assets
      ls /var/cache/redmine/${INSTANCE}/assets/plugin_assets

  Read the instructions in /usr/share/doc/redmine/README.Debian.gz
In particular, look for current instructions about plugins and
the plugin_assets directory.

  Review the web server configuration to see if there are any Alias
directives or similar commands that impact the location of plugin-related
files.  Some of these directives were created for older versions of
Redmine and they may need to be revised or removed for the currently
installed version.

  Try using Rake task assets:reveal to study the list of
files installed by plugins.

  Try re-running Rake tasks assets:precompile and redmine:plugins:migrate
to ensure plugin assets (files) and schema are correctly installed.

  Use strace to see where Ruby is looking for the plugin_assets

  Ask questions in the Redmine forum:

    https://www.redmine.org/projects/redmine/boards

